[Opening "Petroff Defence, Marshall Variation"]
[ECO "C42"]
[Annotator "Frank Marshall - United States chess champion, p.397"]

{In each of his first five games Marshall traded queens by the twenty-first move, as he did in this fourth round encounter.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Nxe5 d6 4. Nf3 Nxe4 5. d4 d5 6. Bd3 Bd6
{At the time this ancient, symmetrical continuation was considered quite inferior to 6...Be7, and Marshall would spend most of his career reviving it.}
7. O-O O-O 8. c4 Be6 9. cxd5 Bxd5 10. Nc3 Nxc3 11. bxc3 Nd7!
{A theoretical novelty, 1900 style. The old move, 11...c5?, had been thoroughly discredited by 12 dxc5 Bxc5 13 Bxh7+! Kxh7 14 Ng5+ and so on. After Marshall's 11...Nd7 the young Austrian analyst Karl Schlechter recommended 12 Re1 and his older Viennese colleague Marco liked 12 Be3 but neither move promises much. As played, however, Marshall eagerly enters an endgame in which White's pawn problems are more significant than his bishop assets.}
12. c4? Bxf3! 13. Qxf3 Qh4 14. Qh3 Qxh3 15. gxh3 c5! 16. d5 Ne5 17. Be2 Rae8 18. f4 b6!
{Almost forced play since move 12. Black's last is a little finesse designed to get his N to d4. The immediate 18...Nc6 19 dxc6 Rxe2 20 cxb7 is not so clear. But now 19 fxe5 Bxe5 20 Rb1 Bd4+ and 21...Rxe2 loses for White.}
19. Bd2 Nc6 20. Bd3 Nd4 21. Kg2 Re7 22. Rae1 Rfe8 23. Rxe7 Rxe7 24. Kf2 Kf8 25. h4 f5!
{In the Wiener Schachzeitung, Marco offered a long analysis of the bishop-of-opposite-color endgame White could force with 26 Be3 g6 27 Bxd4 cxd4 28 Kg3! (28...Re3+ 29 Rf3 Re1 30 Rf1 Rxf1! 31 Bxf1 Ke7) and which he concluded may be lost because Black's more mobile king could penetrate to d4 or c3.}
{For example, 32 Bd3 Bc7! 33 h5 Kd6 34 hxg6 hxg6 35 h4 (35 Kh4 Bd8+) Kc5 36 Bxf5!? gxf5 37 h5 d3 38 Kf3 Bxf4! or 36 h5 gxh5! 37 Bxf5 Kxc4 38 Bg6 h4+ and now 39 Kg4 h3 or 39 Kxh4 Bxf4 wins.}
26. h3 h5 27. Rg1 Kf7 28. Rg5 g6 29. Rg1 Re8 30. Rb1?
{(see diagram)}
{To stop ...a6 and ...b5, White overlooks the main enemy threat.}
30... Be7! 31. Kg3 Bxh4+ 32. Kxh4 Nf3+ 33. Kg3 Nxd2 34. Rd1 Re3+ 35. Kf2 Rxd3 36. Ke2 Rxh3 37. Kxd2 Rf3 38. d6 Rxf4 0-1
{White resigns}
